From 3e82dc19f7cfce1e1fea9e9ac61f1176e0530cba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: axfor <aixiaoxiang2009@hotmail.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 11:17:40 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 39/39] lib/promscrape, lib/streamaggr, app/vmagent: what the
 code review found

- The last acknowledgements on stop went to every target scraped within the
  hour, the pods of a rollout gone since among them, 64 at a time and 5 s each,
  before the stream aggregation's last flush: 130 gone targets held the stop
  for 15 s. They go only to targets scraped within two scrape intervals now,
  and all of them within the timeout.
- The janitor could drop a delta acknowledgement state in use: a scrape storing
  a later second than the janitor's now made the unsigned age wrap around.
- A config reload puts the new aggregators in place and then stops the old
  ones; a push that had loaded the old ones went into them after their last
  flush, lost, or, once they were cleared, matched nothing and was written as
  input. Aggregators.TryPush tells such a push the aggregators stopped, after
  MustStop has waited for the pushes in progress, and remote write pushes it to
  the new ones.
- sample_timestamps writes an interval again at the same timestamp, which a
  storage without -dedup.minScrapeInterval=1ms keeps twice: 250 counted for an
  interval of 150 on a local cluster. Not set, it is on with a single
  -remoteWrite.url and off with several, for the global aggregators too.
- The "final" delta feature is matched as a token, not a substring.

Each has a test that fails on v1.126.1034-cluster and passes now. The same rounds
through the 102 ACG rules give the same 2,887,061 output samples, byte for byte.
